 "strategy": {
  "summary": "Fade (or follow) the shape of the recent move, flat by the close. Price-path convexity (Gulen & Woeppel, JFQA 2025, Purdue) measures when a move happened inside a window: draw a straight line from the window's first close to its last, and see whether the path sagged below it (convex: an up-move came late) or bulged above it (concave: it came early). The paper finds high convexity predicts lower returns on daily data; everything here is intraday, so it is an extrapolation. A plain N-bar reversal runs alongside as the control.",
  "formula": "convexity = ((P1 + PN)/2 \u2212 mean(P1..PN)) / ((P1 + PN)/2); high z \u2192 short, low z \u2192 long",
  "rules": [
   {
    "label": "Signal",
    "text": "Over the last N closes: paper = the formula above; max gap = the single largest line-minus-path gap \u00f7 ATR(14); control = (PN \u2212 P1) \u00f7 ATR(14), the plain N-bar return. Each is turned into a z-score against its own last 25 or 250 bars."
   },
   {
    "label": "Enter",
    "text": "A fresh cross of z above +entry goes short; below \u2212entry goes long, filled at the next bar's open. Quadrant: all = every signal (the paper); late = only fades of the window's net move; early = only trades with it. Optional: the net move must be at least 1, 2 or 3 \u00d7 ATR(14); an EMA(50) trend filter (longs above, shorts below). No entries in the last 30 minutes; a signal while a position is open is dropped."
   },
   {
    "label": "Exit",
    "text": "Time exit after N/2, N or 1.5N bars at that bar's close; optional stop 2.0, 3.0 or 4.0 \u00d7 ATR(14) from the entry, live inside each bar. Anything still open is closed at the session's last close."
   },
   {
    "label": "Size",
    "text": "Long, short or both; one position at a time, never held overnight. As many whole GC contracts as the account covers at full contract value (no leverage), starting from $1,000,000."
   }
  ],
